WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.620
A cut lets in 100 bacteria, and each one doubles every 20 minutes.

2
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.000
New antibodies take days, so what has to happen in the first few hours?

3
00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:17.053
Start with no defense at all.

4
00:00:18.153 --> 00:00:19.873
Every 20 minutes, the count doubles.

5
00:00:21.153 --> 00:00:25.753
The clock runs for four hours, and the counter climbs past 400,000.

6
00:00:26.833 --> 00:00:28.093
Now add a defense.

7
00:00:29.473 --> 00:00:32.993
In each doubling time, it removes a fraction of the bacteria.

8
00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:35.573
Call that fraction k.

9
00:00:36.773 --> 00:00:39.233
The survivors then double, just as before.

10
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:44.833
So each doubling time multiplies the count by 2 × (1 − k).

11
00:00:46.073 --> 00:00:47.733
That factor is the whole race.

12
00:00:49.113 --> 00:00:50.293
Above 1, the count grows.

13
00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:52.813
Below 1, it shrinks.

14
00:00:53.817 --> 00:00:58.957
Suppose the defenses remove three quarters of the bacteria every 20 minutes.

15
00:01:00.277 --> 00:01:01.677
Does the count rise or fall?

16
00:01:03.037 --> 00:01:04.577
Decide before the counter moves.

17
00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:16.803
It falls. The factor is 1/2, so every 20 minutes the pile halves.

18
00:01:18.163 --> 00:01:22.363
After four hours, the expected count is far below one bacterium.

19
00:01:23.523 --> 00:01:25.543
The infection is very likely cleared.

20
00:01:26.617 --> 00:01:28.277
Now slide the dial down.

21
00:01:29.517 --> 00:01:33.437
At exactly one half removed the factor is 1, and the counter freezes.

22
00:01:34.877 --> 00:01:39.657
So in this model, the rule is simple: remove more than half every doubling time.

23
00:01:40.717 --> 00:01:42.537
But when does the removal start?

24
00:01:43.757 --> 00:01:46.737
Keep the same strong defense and make it wait one hour.

25
00:01:47.997 --> 00:01:50.257
The pile reaches 800 before it turns.

26
00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:54.527
Wait two hours, and it reaches 6,400.

27
00:01:56.047 --> 00:01:58.907
Each hour of waiting multiplies the pile eightfold.

28
00:02:00.227 --> 00:02:03.547
That is why the receptors must be ready before infection.

29
00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:09.507
In this model of a bacterial wound, the defenses we follow do three jobs.

30
00:02:10.947 --> 00:02:12.027
First, fewer get in.

31
00:02:13.327 --> 00:02:15.907
Skin and other barriers lower the starting number.

32
00:02:16.967 --> 00:02:18.867
Second, the clock starts early.

33
00:02:20.107 --> 00:02:25.127
A macrophage, a long-lived eating cell in the tissue, carries ready-made receptors.

34
00:02:26.547 --> 00:02:31.787
One, Toll-like receptor 4 senses a molecule from the outer membrane of many

35
00:02:31.787 --> 00:02:32.787
bacteria.

36
00:02:33.467 --> 00:02:35.667
Third, more are removed per doubling.

37
00:02:37.167 --> 00:02:38.487
Sensing sets off inflammation.

38
00:02:39.947 --> 00:02:41.747
A nearby vessel widens and leaks.

39
00:02:43.287 --> 00:02:47.167
Neutrophils pour out first, then monocytes, which become macrophages.

40
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:54.480
Inflammation runs in four steps: sense, vessels change, cells arrive, then clear and

41
00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:00.200
repair. As the eating cells gather, the fraction removed in each doubling time

42
00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:01.200
climbs.

43
00:03:01.733 --> 00:03:06.233
Keep them out, see them at once, and remove more than half every doubling time.

44
00:03:07.473 --> 00:03:11.513
This is an illustrative model of one wound, not a measured infection.
